s the titleist  derived from my first interview with  rickover  was he tough there's no way to describe  how tough he was yes and and i think  that  the chief executive officer general  electric  company was just as uh nervous when  rickover was on the way disconnected in  new york as i was  recover was very tough but fair i think  the finest engineer has ever lived  and personally responsible for the  evolution of nuclear power as a peaceful  peacetime use does  the personal toughness of an individual  does that work  i mean did  he scare you  yeah he scared me i mean have you ever  used that with others  my three sons say that i used it with  them  but usually i'm a lot more easygoing  than that and certainly with advanced as  advancing age i've become a lot more  mellow in my attitude toward others  and uh i would think that most people  would say that i have a general approach  to other folks now  but this being tough work  i think so in a in a in a final  mediation or negotiation you have to be  extremely tough you have to know exactly  how far you can go and go no further  and uh and you have to use the maximum  degree of persuasion to induce  a recalcitrant recalcitrant party to  accept your proposal yes i think you  have to be tough  a poem here  with this illustration by your  granddaughter you say is now  17 yes she going to go on to be a  professional i think she's still she's  in a very advanced high school in san  francisco  that has a very good art department so i  don't have any doubt about it by the way  that poem is the only sonnet in the poem  i found it very difficult to write a  sonnet what's a sonnet a sonnet is a  classical format for a poem that was  used repeatedly by shakespeare and which  is a  as a very specific uh  frame work both uh for emphasis on words  and rhythm and length of lines and they  and the style of rhyming  it's a it's a rigid form that can't be  from which you cannot deviate i still  call it the sonnet  i can still remember and i'd love to you  can tell me where i can find this a memo  that was written by ham jordan  in 19  tell me if i'm wrong about this 72 in  december  it ended up in a book that i remember  reading that laid out your campaign  in which planes was kind of like the  staging area  uh  do you remember that memo well i  remember the memo i didn't realize it  had been published in a book though i  think marty sram had a book out well he  might have well you know there were  probably 30 books written about that  remarkable campaign  and maybe that marty schramm didn't  quote from it i didn't remember that can  anybody ever do that again use a small  town in a place like georgia and  well hope arkansas is not much yeah but  he didn't go back there all the time no  i didn't didn't have uh trailers with  network cameras well planes you know is  is now and always has been the center of  my life when i was even in the navy you  know i there was a magnet pulling me  back to planes as i said earlier  both my ancestors and roseanne's who  were born in the 1700s are buried there  and we've  we've got the same land that's been in  our family since 1832.  and  you know in our both of our families are  there  so there's a strong magnetism pulling us  back to play so plains is the center of  our life  and i don't i doubt it that very many  people in this modern fast changing  technological mobile world  has that uh intensity of attraction to a  small town where a husband and a wife  both are from there and feel compelled  you know pleasantly to go back how far  is it from atlanta  by airplane it's 120 miles exactly to  south by road it's about 130 miles it  takes what how long to drive well we  allot three hours to go from planes to  the carter center and that allows  one one stop to go the restroom and  without rushing  to go from from planes to the airport we  a lot uh two hours and a half  if one travels if someone goes to planes  right now to see you or to see the town  what's there  that picture in the uh and the sonnet is  exactly what's there now it's what was  there in 1900 do you own that thing's  there now still do you still have them  i own i own yes i own all the land that  we've ever owned a couple of thousand  acres and we own a house on a farm  that's that's just barely inside the  city limits of plains is there a tour  that one takes now yes there is  people telling you that jimmy carter is  from here exactly yes it's run at the  park services uh plains is now  a national historical site and this park  service is developing  some of the tourist attractions there  but there's a there's a guy that that  owns billy carter's old service station  who uh who has a tour i think it costs  five bucks  and you can go around and visit 20  different sites and planes that have  something to do with me or family  what's this illustration about that's a  illustration from a poem called peanuts  that describes life in a small town  during the 1930s  and  that's me with a bag of  with a basket full of peanuts that i'm  taking to planes to sell i used to walk  down the railroad track about two miles  and a half  to planes and sell 20 bags of peanuts  for a total of a dollar and then brought  back home let me read this paragraph  okay almost ignored an omnipresent boy i  learned how merchants cheat  which married men laid half a dollar  [ __ ] not always white  the same ones touting racial purity and  klansmen sheeted bravery at night  what's that all about  well that's actually a fact  in the first place as a five six seven  year eight eight-year-old boy  omnipresent on the plane streets every  day  the adults  learned just to ignore my presence  and they would say things and do things  as though i was not there  and some of the men would uh would talk  about their sexual exploits  or some of the ones that were more  i'd say disreputable or whatever  would brag about going down to albany  georgia 35 miles away to a whorehouse  and having sex with