he poem  to roseland because it was so  personal and emotional that it that in  effect it embarrassed him to read it but  but that has turned out to be  i think the favorite poem in the whole  book  when i signed books and i signed  maybe 1500 to 2000 at each bookstore  a lot of the people will open  the book to a certain point and ask me  to sign it there instead of on a fly  leaf  and  a surprising portion of the women want  me to sign the roseland  point  here's a  a reflection of the beauty in washington  but it's the illustration that i also  want to show has your granddaughter been  here often  i don't think she's ever been here  so how does she figure out to do  something like well she knows she knows  what the  capitol looks like actually i was in the  white house  and they were on the roof of the white  house i think the white house shows up  at yes  and i think the poem is one of the most  beautiful of all it's  it's a very simple poem  about our going to the roof of the white  house one night my youngest son was an  amateur astronomer  and he wanted us to look at a  constellation  and  we were aggravated because  you couldn't see the stars very clearly  because of the washington city lights  and all of a sudden we heard this  primeval  sound coming from the north  and there was a beautiful flight of  geese going over washington and the city  lights were being reflected from their  breasts  and it was one of the most memorable and  beautiful sights i've ever seen and  that's what i wrote the poem about  you have a phone poem that has the  heading some things i love and  things like my in basket empty  a good new book yes now fiction  nonfiction both  best book you've read in the last few  months  most interesting  well uh uh the not the fiction book i've  read recently i liked it by a danish  writer and smile and her knowledge of  the snow  ice and snow  i think that one of the most interesting  non-fiction books i've read recently is  the quark and the jaguar which is a a  book about scientific discoveries  and the recent book about franklin and  eleanor roosevelt i found to be  interesting  the end of a six mile run in the rain  that's right some things i love yes why  you still run yes i ran this morning  sure  i run whenever possible when we're  traveling overseas my wife and i run  every morning quite early we get up  early we run  uh when it gets daybreak and uh we  generally get through before sunrise  and then we can go through the rest of  the day on you know in restaurants and  in meetings and on airplanes and we  still enjoy running grandchildren coming  in our front door the same ones leaving  in a day or two  what what  [Music]  what's the best thing that somebody can  say about you the best thing what do you  like what you know as you travel around  i'm sure people say  things to your face that you like to  hear and maybe some things you don't  like to hear but what's the what do you  want to i don't want to say be  remembered by but what do you what do  you like  the most well i don't know it's hard to  say  oh i think historically i'd like to be  remembered for things that we did  that contributed to peace and human  rights  i'd like  people to  understand it that i've  been honest and  truthful  uh and that i've loved the simplest  things of life i'd like for  to be remembered as a good grandfather  we have nine grandchildren you know  there's a wide range of things i'd like  to be remembered for i was an  outstanding farmer  at one time i produced the best seed  peanuts i think in this country perhaps  in the world i was very proud of that i  was a good submarine also so yeah the  different phases in my life i've been  i've wanted to be remembered for  different things  plan to write another book  i'm writing another book right now and  as i say i put it i've published two  books since this one was begun  uh whether i'll have a uh  have another book of poetry i don't yet  know  i've got seven or eight poems now that  are in the development stage  and so i continue to write poetry i  really enjoy this  i've been amazed really at how  uh much of a self-revelation comes from  a poem  you know i i start writing a poem about  things that that i want to treat  superficially in a way  and before i know it i've  kind of exploring the the inner depth of  my soul and my consciousness and my  memory and  and  revealing things that otherwise i would  never have told anyone  this is what the cover of the book looks  like  a book of poems always a reckoning  by jimmy carter thank you very much for  your time i've enjoyed it very much  you've been watching a 1995 encore book  notes interview with former president  jimmy carter his first book of poetry  always a reckoning is published by  random house for more information visit  their website randomhouse.com  next week on encore book notes winston s  churchill the grandson of sir winston  churchill sat down with us
 

